{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Chasing Cherry Blossoms in Kyoto**\n\nLast spring, I finally made the leap and booked a ticket to Japan. My destination? The ancient capital of Kyoto. From the moment I stepped off the Shinkansen, the air felt different—crisp, clean, and filled with the faint scent of incense.\n\nMy first stop was the Fushimi Inari Taisha. Walking through thousands of vermilion torii gates winding up the mountain was surreal. The sunlight filtered through the pillars, creating a dance of shadows that felt almost spiritual. Hikers bowed politely as we passed, a small gesture that highlighted the deep respect embedded in the culture.\n\nThe next day, I wandered through the Arashiyama Bamboo Grove. The stalks towered overhead, swaying gently in the wind with a creaking sound that locals say is one of Japan's most beautiful natural melodies. Later, I sat by the Kamo River, watching locals fly kites and families enjoy picnics under blooming sakura trees. The petals drifted down like pink snow, landing softly on the water's surface.\n\nFood was, of course, a highlight. I devoured steaming bowls of ramen in narrow alleyways, sampled fresh matcha sweets in traditional tea houses, and even tried my hand at making sushi at a local market. Every bite was a lesson in precision and flavor.\n\nLeaving Japan was bittersweet. The blend of ultra-modern technology and preserved tradition creates a unique rhythm that stays with you long after you return home. If you ever get the chance to visit, don't just see the sights; take a moment to breathe in the atmosphere. It's an experience that truly changes your perspective.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.051065892}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.13473757}, {"token": "asing", "logprob": -0.7864187}, {"token": " Cherry", "logprob": -2.121948e-05}, {"token": " Bloss", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": "oms", "logprob": 0.0}, {"